{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CfaXkTKDiuNtZb9phuFVE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe decomposition is genuinely good: validation lives in its own `ValidateOwnerFields` step, `BuildOwner` is correctly reduced to a pure `@Val`-consuming mapper (the only `@RequestBody` binding moved to the first step, per the \"body read once\" rule), the checked exception + escalation handler + YAML wiring all follow the documented pattern, and the error-collection logic (accumulate all offending fields, then throw once) matches the spec's \"list each missing field\" requirement. One structural issue:\n\n**`govern: [ transaction ]` on the `validate` step is out of place.**\n- `src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`: the new `validate` step carries `govern: [ transaction ]`, but it performs no persistence — it only binds, checks blanks, and either throws or republishes the DTO. The project's own worked example in AGENTS.md (`PUT /owners/{ownerId}`) deliberately does **not** govern the `validate` (or `load`) step with a transaction; only the write steps (`apply`, `save`) list it. AGENTS.md is explicit that governance should be \"listed on **every** step it covers\" — i.e. the write steps — not on pure pre-checks.\n- Beyond being non-idiomatic, it contradicts the intent of validating first: the point of running validation up front is to fail fast *before* doing any transactional work, yet this begins a write transaction only to roll it back on the common rejection path. Drop `govern: [ transaction ]` from the `validate` step so transaction governance starts at `build`/the write steps, matching the documented shape."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11441,"cache_read_input_tokens":11894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens"